---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Help | Time Zone Guru"
  description="How to compare cities, read overlap, handle daylight saving, and edit the sleep/work/awake template."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main class="site-main content-page">
    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Help</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Time Zone Guru shows the same moment in several places at once, so you
        can spot when people are likely free, even when daylight saving rules
        differ.
      </p>

      <h2>Quick start</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Now</strong> (top row) shows the live clock in each city.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Add or remove cities</strong> with search and the chips.
          Defaults are your current location, London, and New York (duplicates
          of your zone are skipped).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rows</strong> are hours of your local day; columns are each city’s
          wall time at that same moment.
        </li>
        <li>
          The <strong>Good</strong> column (emoji + green) shows how many cities are awake.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>How to read the schedule</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Now</strong> is always live wall time, independent of the selected date.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your location</strong> is the first column: hours of your local day,
          with a short zone label (e.g. <code>GMT+1</code>, <code>EDT</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rows = hours</strong> of your local day on the chosen date.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Columns = locations</strong>. Each cell is that city’s local
          time at the same absolute moment as your hour.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>+1 / −1</strong> badges mean that city is already on the next or
          previous calendar day.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Colors</h2>
      <p>
        For each of your local hours we count how many selected cities are
        <em>awake</em> (work or free-time in the day template).
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Who’s free / Good</strong> (row color + emoji from 🌒 to ☀️): how
          good the hour is for everyone (hover for the exact count).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>In each city</strong> (💤 ⚒️ ☕): sleep / work / awake from the day
          template for that place’s local hour.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Daylight saving &amp; offsets</h2>
      <p>
        Times use the browser’s IANA timezone database via <code>Intl</code>.
        When you pick a date in March or November, offsets update automatically
        for zones that observe DST. Melbourne and New York do not switch on the
        same weekend, for example.
      </p>
      <p>
        City names come from a GeoNames-based extract (larger cities worldwide plus
        each country’s main places), each mapped to an IANA zone id. Offsets are
        never invented by hand.
      </p>

      <h2>Sleep / work / awake template</h2>
      <p>
        Open <strong>Typical day</strong> at the bottom of the compare tool. That
        template is matched to each city’s <em>local</em> hour (not yours). Defaults
        assume night sleep, daytime work, evening awake, which you edit freely.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>24-hour clock</strong>: midnight at the top, hours clockwise,
          one slice per hour (15°). Drag the ring to paint.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>AM / PM strips</strong>: two rows of twelve hours for precise
          taps.
        </li>
        <li>Presets: workday 09–17, all sleep, all awake, or reset.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Share a comparison</h2>
      <p>
        Under the schedule, use <strong>Copy link</strong> (or <strong>Share…</strong> on
        supported phones). The link encodes selected cities in the URL. Optionally include
        the date and your sleep/work/awake day template. Anyone with the link sees that
        comparison. No account required.
      </p>
      <p>
        Example:
        <code>?z=Europe/London,America/New_York&amp;d=2026-07-15</code>
      </p>

      <h2>Tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Search by city, country, abbreviation, or IANA id (e.g. <code>Tokyo</code>,
          <code>UK</code>, <code>USA</code> / <code>America</code>, <code>NYC</code>,
          <code>Europe/Paris</code>). Type more letters to narrow long result lists.
        </li>
        <li>Remove a city by tapping its chip.</li>
        <li>Use the <strong>12h / 24h</strong> switch next to the date control.</li>
        <li>
          Many cities scroll sideways in the grid; the first two columns stay put.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        More background on why this exists:
        <a href="/about/">About</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
